Everything is clean. Here's a summary of what was implemented:

  ---
  What was added

  src/backends/boundary.jl (new file, ~350 lines) — three exported symbols and
  three internal helpers:

  boundary_cluster(x, y; shrink=0.5) -> Vector{Int}

  MATLAB boundary(x, y, shrink) analogue. Algorithm:
  1. Builds the Delaunay triangulation via the existing DelaunayTriangulation.jl
   dependency
  2. Computes circumradius for every solid triangle using _circumradius_2d
  (@inline, @inbounds)
  3. Sets alpha_radius = quantile(circumradii, 1−shrink) — at shrink=0 all
  triangles are included (convex hull shortcut via get_convex_hull_vertices); at
   shrink=1 only the smallest triangle is kept
  4. Extracts boundary edges (those belonging to exactly one included triangle)
  using a pre-sized Dict{Tuple{Int,Int},Int}
  5. Traces all closed loops; returns the largest in CCW order (shoelace
  sign-check + reverse) 6. Returns a closed Vector{Int} (first == last), same convention as MATLAB BoundaryClustersConfig(; shrink=0.5, per_dataset=true)
  Plain struct with two knobs. per_dataset should match the value used in the
  original cluster() call.

  boundary_clusters(smld_out, cfg) -> (smld_out, ClusterBoundaryInfo)

  Read-only, pass-through SMLD (like cluster_statistics). Groups emitters by
  (dataset, cluster_id) pairs, calls boundary_cluster per cluster, returns
  per-cluster boundary polygons as (boundaries_x[j], boundaries_y[j]) coordinate
   vectors in μm.

  Performance: O(n log n) dominated by DT triangulation (~2.5 ms/cluster at
  n=200; same cost as existing Voronoi backend). All inner loops are type-stable
   with concrete types and @inbounds. 257 pre-existing tests continue to pass.

